= Distribution shift — Technical =

== Distribution factorization ==
Using:

<pre>
P(X,Y) = P(Y|X)P(X)
       = P(X|Y)P(Y)
</pre>

different invariance assumptions define shift models.

=== Covariate shift ===

<pre>
P_s(X) ≠ P_t(X)
P_s(Y|X) = P_t(Y|X)
</pre>

=== Label shift ===

<pre>
P_s(Y) ≠ P_t(Y)
P_s(X|Y) = P_t(X|Y)
</pre>

Other conditional/concept shifts alter the predictive relationship itself.

== Identifiability ==
Shift correction generally requires assumptions.

Without labels or structural assumptions in the target domain, multiple target distributions can be observationally compatible.

== Importance weighting ==
Under covariate shift, target risk can sometimes be expressed through source samples weighted by density ratio:

<pre>
w(x) = p_t(x) / p_s(x)
</pre>

when support/estimation assumptions hold.

